        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "Isocoria: the normal pupillary condition in which both pupils are equal in size and react to light by dilating or constricting.\nMydriasis: both pupils are dilated. This commonly occurs due to substance intoxication and may also be a sign of brain injury. The pupils do not react to light.\nMiosis: both pupils are markedly constricted, generally caused by opioid use, chemical intoxication, or it may also indicate brain injury. The pupils do not react to light.\nAnisocoria: inequality in pupil size, which may indicate brain injury. The pupils do not react to light.\nThe pupillary reflex is an assessment measure used in the Glasgow Coma Scale to help determine whether there is impairment of neurological function."
        },
